  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric properties of a regular 24-gon and the conditions for drawing 12 segments of equal lengths such that each vertex of the 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Understanding the Regular 24-gon:**\n   - A regular 24-gon has 24 vertices, labeled \\( V_1, V_2, \\ldots, V_{24} \\).\n   - Each vertex is connected to its neighboring vertices by segments of equal length.\n\n2. **Drawing 12 Segments:**\n   - We need to draw 12 segments such that each of the 24 vertices is an endpoint of exactly one segment.\n   - This means we need to partition the 24 vertices into 12 pairs, where each pair is connected by a segment of equal length.\n\n3. **Partitioning the Vertices:**\n   - We need to find the number of ways to pair up the 24 vertices.\n   - The number of ways to pair up \\( n \\) vertices is given by \\( \\frac{(n-1)!}{2^{(n-1)/2} \\left(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right)!} \\).\n\n4. **Calculating the Number of Ways:**\n   - For \\( n = 24 \\), we need to calculate \\( \\frac{23!}{2^{12} \\cdot 12!} \\).\n\n5. **Simplifying the Expression:**\n   - The expression \\( \\frac{23!}{2^{12} \\cdot 12!} \\) can be simplified to \\( \\frac{23 \\cdot 22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 11}{2^{12}} \\).\n\n6. **Final Calculation:**\n   - The number of ways to pair up the 24 vertices is \\( \\frac{23 \\cdot 22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 11}{2^{12}} \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to draw 12 segments of equal lengths such that each vertex of the regular 24-gon is an endpoint of exactly one of the 12 segments is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{23 \\cdot 22 \\cdot 21 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 16 \\cdot 15 \\cdot 14 \\cdot 13 \\cdot 12}{2^{12}}}\n\\]",
